  },
  "company_name": "Cadence",
  "slug": "cadence",
  "stage": "seed",
  "sector": "Field-Service Scheduling SaaS",
  "geography": "US",
  "sector_type": "saas"
}

{
  "assessment_mode": "sub-agent",
  "partner_verdicts": [
    {
      "partner": "visionary",
      "verdict": "more_diligence",
      "rationale": "Cadence shows the kind of early growth signal that gets a visionary's attention: 18% month-over-month growth compounding for four consecutive months on $480K ARR is not noise \u2014 it suggests genuine pull. In a market where millions of HVAC, plumbing, landscaping, and electrical SMBs still run operations on whiteboards and spreadsheets, the digitization wave has barely started for the long tail. The 80% gross margins confirm this is a software business, not a services business dressed up as one.\n\nBut the visionary's core questions remain unanswered. Field-service management is not a new category \u2014 ServiceTitan is a $9B+ company, Jobber raised over $100M, and Housecall Pro, FieldEdge, and others are well-capitalized and entrenched. The materials describe Cadence as 'scheduling and dispatch in one place' \u2014 that is exactly what every incumbent says. The critical question for a 10-year investment thesis is not whether there is a market, but whether Cadence is building a new paradigm or fighting for share in a crowded one.\n\nThe most intriguing unanswered angle is the founding team's DNA. An ex-Stripe payments-infrastructure engineer paired with an ex-Notion product-growth PM is a combination that could point toward something larger: an embedded financial services layer for field service businesses \u2014 think payments, financing, insurance \u2014 built on top of the scheduling workflow, the way Toast built a restaurant operating system on top of POS. If that is the 10-year vision, Cadence could be creating a new category, not just another FSM SaaS. But that vision is not in the materials, and the visionary needs to hear it directly from the founders before committing capital.\n\nThe absence of a commercial co-founder is a flag worth naming: SMB field service is sold neighborhood by neighborhood, through trade associations and local relationships. Two technical co-founders can build the product, but the visionary needs conviction that the go-to-market motion can scale beyond product-led growth before this round closes."
    },
    {
      "partner": "operator",
      "verdict": "more_diligence",
      "rationale": "Cadence shows genuine early execution: 120 paying customers, $40K MRR, 18% month-over-month growth sustained over four months, and a CAC of $1,200 against roughly $333 monthly ARPA at 80% gross margins. The implied CAC payback period is approximately 4-5 months \u2014 excellent by any benchmark, and a signal that something is working in how they're reaching customers. That's real. I'm not dismissing it.\n\nBut here is where my concern sits: the two founders are a Stripe engineer and a Notion PM. Those are strong individual backgrounds, but neither maps to the buyer they are selling to \u2014 SMB service business owners running HVAC, plumbing, landscaping, pest control operations. Field-service SMBs are a notoriously difficult-to-reach segment that skews toward owner-operators who buy on trust, referral, and trade relationships, not inbound SaaS funnels. There is no commercial co-founder, no GTM hire mentioned, and nothing in the materials that explains HOW they've acquired 120 customers at $1,200 CAC. That number is good, but I need to know whether it's repeatable \u2014 whether there's a playbook, a channel, a motion \u2014 or whether these are founder-network sales that collapse the moment they try to scale beyond their personal reach.\n\nThe growth rate compounds the urgency of understanding the GTM motion. 18% MoM for four consecutive months means the customer count and MRR are doubling roughly every four months. If that's running on a channel with real leverage, this is a serious seed-stage company. If it's running on founder hustle, they're going to hit a wall right around the time they close this round and need to hire. I also have no churn data. SMB customers fail, switch, and churn at rates that can hollow out a 'growing' ARR book in 12-18 months. Without retention curves, I cannot assess the real LTV side of any unit economics calculation. These are gaps I can close in diligence \u2014 they are not, by themselves, reasons to pass \u2014 but I will not invest without them."
    },
    {
      "partner": "analyst",
      "verdict": "more_diligence",
      "rationale": "Cadence presents a number of genuinely interesting financial signals at the seed stage, but the materials lack the retention and burn data I need to underwrite this deal with conviction. Starting with what works: an 80% gross margin meets the SaaS benchmark and suggests the infrastructure cost base is well-managed. The CAC of $1,200 against an ARPA of approximately $333/month implies a payback period of roughly 4.5 months on a fully-loaded gross-margin basis \u2014 if that holds under scrutiny, it is one of the most capital-efficient acquisition economics I have seen at this stage. The $40K MRR with 120 customers and 18% MoM growth over four consecutive months is directionally encouraging and suggests early repeatability. However, the materials disclose zero churn or retention data. Without that, I cannot compute LTV with any defensible basis, and the LTV/CAC ratio \u2014 the metric I care about most at seed \u2014 is undefined. I also have no burn rate or runway figure, which means I cannot assess whether the $4M ask is sized appropriately relative to milestones, or whether the capital efficiency implied by the low CAC translates to responsible burn. The $16M pre-money valuation at $480K ARR represents approximately a 33x ARR multiple, which is at the upper end of seed pricing and requires both clean retention curves and a credible path to fund-returning scale to justify. The ARPA of roughly $4,000 per year is low for vertical SaaS \u2014 at that price point, the business needs high customer volume and expansion revenue (NDR well above 100%) to compound toward venture-scale outcomes. On balance, the disclosed metrics are promising enough to keep this alive, but I will not move past more diligence without retention cohorts, an NDR figure, a burn rate, and runway to next-round milestones."
    }
  ],
  "debate_sections": [
    {
      "topic": "Responses to Visionary",
      "exchanges": [
        {
          "partner": "operator",
          "position": "The Toast analogy is the most interesting thing I've heard in this room, but I have to hold my position. The ex-Stripe payments infrastructure background and the ex-Notion product-growth background ARE the raw material for an embedded fintech play \u2014 I don't dispute that. What I can't do is let a plausible 10-year narrative substitute for evidence of present execution. The visionary is asking me to believe that two founders with no field-service operating experience can (a) acquire SMB owner-operators at scale through whatever channel has produced 120 customers so far, and (b) eventually layer in payments, financing, and insurance on top of a scheduling workflow \u2014 all without a commercial co-founder and without disclosing a single detail about how they reach customers today. The Toast comparison actually cuts against them: Toast built on top of deep restaurant-industry relationships and a team that understood POS sales motions to independent operators. What's the equivalent here? I need to see the GTM motion before I can underwrite the embedded fintech thesis, not after."
        },
        {
          "partner": "analyst",
          "position": "The embedded payments thesis built on the ex-Stripe background is speculative narrative, not evidence. Nothing in the startup materials indicates Cadence is building or planning a financial services layer \u2014 that's a hypothesis the visionary is constructing from the team's prior employer, not something the company has disclosed. I'm not dismissing the optionality, but I can't underwrite a 33x ARR multiple on the strength of a hypothetical product roadmap. What I need to see is whether the $4,000/year ARPA has any expansion path TODAY \u2014 are customers buying more seats, adding modules, moving up-tier? If NDR is north of 110%, the visionary's embedded-payments thesis becomes financially plausible to stress-test. Without that data, it remains a story, not a signal."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "topic": "Responses to Operator",
      "exchanges": [
        {
          "partner": "visionary",
          "position": "The operator's concern about the GTM motion being completely undisclosed is real and I share it \u2014 I flagged the absence of a commercial co-founder for exactly this reason. But I want to complicate the framing: the operator treats this as an execution problem (is the channel repeatable?), and that is the right question at the seed stage. My concern is upstream of that \u2014 even if the CAC of $1,200 at 4-5 months payback holds under scrutiny, it answers *how* they acquire customers, not *why this is the right moment to build* and *what paradigm shift Cadence is riding*. A good GTM motion on top of a me-too product in a crowded market is a lifestyle business, not a category winner. I want to hear the vision before I stress-test the channel. That said, the operator is right that the churn data gap is more diagnostic than I gave it credit for in round one \u2014 a 18% MoM growth headline with hollow cohort retention beneath it is a very different investment than the same headline with strong retention, and the materials disclose nothing to distinguish between the two. I'm adding that to my diligence list."
        },
        {
          "partner": "analyst",
          "position": "The operator identifies something I did not explicitly flag in round one: the founding team's backgrounds \u2014 payments infrastructure and product growth \u2014 do not map to the buyer persona of HVAC owner-operators and plumbers who buy on trust and trade relationships. That is a real and specific concern about whether the $1,200 CAC is repeatable at scale or is a function of founder-network sales that collapse the moment they try to hire a sales rep. I'm adding channel attribution by customer type to my diligence requirements as a direct result of this point. However, it does not change my verdict: this concern, like my core financial data gaps, lands in 'more diligence needed' territory rather than 'pass.' A 4.5-month implied CAC payback from founder-led sales is still a data point worth investigating rather than dismissing \u2014 the question is whether any repeatable non-founder channel exists underneath it."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "topic": "Responses to Analyst",
      "exchanges": [
        {
          "partner": "visionary",
          "position": "The analyst's point that the $16M pre-money at $480K ARR implies a roughly 33x ARR multiple is accurate and I won't dispute the arithmetic. But the analyst and I weigh it differently. At seed stage, I do not value a company on current ARR multiples \u2014 I value it on the trajectory of that ARR and the size of the market it can address. If Cadence is genuinely on a path to embed financial services in the field-service workflow \u2014 payments, financing, insurance \u2014 then $480K ARR is noise in a $20B+ serviceable market, and 33x today might look like a gift in three years. The analyst is right to flag it as a pricing risk, but I would weight that risk very differently if the founders can articulate a compelling answer to 'what does Cadence look like in 2036?' The analyst's concern becomes decisive only if the vision is incremental. The burn rate gap is also a legitimate flag \u2014 not because I think they are burning recklessly, but because I want to understand whether the $4M ask is structured around a specific set of milestones that unlock the next conversation, or whether it is simply 'what felt right.' That is a question I will bring into the IC session."
        },
        {
          "partner": "operator",
          "position": "I agree with the analyst that the absence of churn and retention data is disqualifying for conviction \u2014 my own diligence requirements say the same. On the 33x ARR multiple at $16M pre-money: the analyst is right that this is at the upper end of seed pricing, and I won't pretend otherwise. But as an operator, the multiple concern is secondary to whether the CAC payback is real. If the GTM motion holds up and the ~4-5 month payback survives scrutiny, a 33x multiple at seed for a vertical SaaS with 18% MoM growth and 80% gross margins is defensible \u2014 this is not egregiously priced if the unit economics are verified. The analyst's structural point about low ARPA ($4K/year) creating a volume challenge is well-taken: at $333/month per customer, you need thousands of customers to build an ARR base that matters. That compounds my concern about whether the current acquisition motion scales \u2014 founder-network sales at $1,200 CAC might work for the first 120 customers; it will not work for the first 1,200. We are aligned on needing cohort data. Where I hold firm: the burn rate disclosure gap the analyst flagged is important but is in our shared diligence list, and the multiple is not my primary gating item here."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "consensus_verdict": "more_diligence",
  "debated_dealbreakers": [],
  "key_concerns": [
    "The materials provide no 'why now' narrative. ServiceTitan, Jobber, and Housecall Pro have been attacking this market for over a decade with hundreds of millions in capital. A seed-stage entrant in 2026 needs a crisp macro catalyst \u2014 AI-native scheduling that incumbents cannot retrofit, a regulatory shift, a behavioral change in how SMB owners hire and manage crews \u2014 and none is articulated. Without it, the visionary cannot distinguish a new category from a late-market entry.",
    "The one-liner ('manage jobs, crews, and customers in one place') is feature-level positioning, not category-level vision. If Cadence is building toward an embedded financial services or AI operations platform, that story needs to be told now \u2014 investors who funded ServiceTitan and Jobber will hear 'FSM SaaS for SMBs' and pass on pattern recognition alone. The vision has to be differentiated enough to reframe the competitive map.",
    "A two-person technical founding team with no commercial co-founder is a structural risk for a business that sells to SMB field service operators. This customer segment is acquired through trade shows, franchise networks, local referrals, and direct outreach \u2014 not through product-led growth alone. The materials do not address how the founding team plans to build the GTM motion, and at 120 customers the early channels may not reveal the ceiling yet.",
    "No non-technical co-founder and no commercial GTM leader on the team. A Stripe infrastructure engineer and a Notion PM are strong product-builders, but neither background maps to the buyer \u2014 SMB owner-operators in trades and field services. B2B GTM into this segment requires deep channel intuition, trade-specific trust signals, and often a founder who has lived the problem from the operator side. Without a commercial co-founder or an early GTM hire, scaling beyond founder-led sales into a repeatable motion is a meaningful execution risk for a team raising on a $16M pre-money.",
    "The GTM motion is completely undisclosed. 120 customers at $1,200 CAC is a good outcome, but the materials describe none of the 'how.' What is the primary acquisition channel \u2014 inbound, outbound, referral, partnerships with trade associations? Is there a repeatable sales playbook? Who closes deals and how long does the sales cycle run? Without this, I cannot assess whether the growth rate is a function of a scalable channel or founder hustle that will not transfer to a sales team.",
    "No churn or retention data provided. SMB customers in field-service trades are among the highest-churn cohorts in vertical SaaS \u2014 businesses close, cash flow tightens, owners switch tools. Without cohort-level retention curves, I have no ability to assess true LTV. A 4-5 month CAC payback on paper becomes unsustainable if monthly churn is running above 3-4%, which is not unusual for SMB tools at early stages.",
    "Competitive environment is dense and well-funded. ServiceTitan has raised over $1.5B and owns the premium end of the field-service market. Jobber and Housecall Pro have significant SMB penetration with established distribution. The materials do not articulate what specifically Cadence does differently \u2014 a 'one place' value proposition is table stakes, not differentiation, and without a clear moat or wedge the company is competing on execution alone against entrenched incumbents.",
    "Zero retention or churn data disclosed. Without monthly or annual churn rates and cohort curves, LTV is undefined, NDR is unknown, and I have no way to determine whether growth is compounding on a durable base or being masked by high early-stage churn. High NDR can also mask high base churn \u2014 I need both GRR and NDR.",
    "No burn rate or runway disclosed. The $4M ask at a $16M pre-money valuation implies a specific capital deployment thesis, but without knowing current burn, I cannot assess whether this round covers 18-24 months to the next milestone or burns through in 9 months with nothing to show for it.",
    "Low ARPA of approximately $4,000/year for a vertical SaaS targeting SMBs creates a structural volume challenge: to generate a fund-returning outcome from a $16M pre-money entry, the business likely needs tens of thousands of paying customers or a meaningful expansion revenue motion (NDR >120%). Neither is evidenced in the materials.",
    "The $16M pre-money valuation at $480K ARR is approximately 33x ARR, which requires clean retention and a credible growth trajectory to sustain in the next round at a mark-up. If retention turns out to be weak, the founders will face a significant re-pricing risk at Series A."
  ],
  "diligence_requirements": [
    "A clear articulation of the differentiated 10-year vision \u2014 specifically whether the founders see an embedded financial services layer (payments, financing, insurance) built on top of the scheduling workflow, or a pure FSM SaaS competing on features.",
    "A 'why now' narrative grounded in a specific macro catalyst \u2014 regulatory change, technology inflection, or behavioral shift that makes 2024-2025 the right moment to build Cadence rather than 2015 when ServiceTitan and Jobber were already attacking this market.",
    "Customer acquisition breakdown by channel for the 120 customers \u2014 which channels have the most velocity and whether the GTM motion is founder-network-dependent or structurally repeatable.",
    "Monthly cohort retention data \u2014 the operator and analyst are both right that 18% MoM growth without retention curves is an incomplete picture; a visionary needs to know whether the product compounds or churns.",
    "Burn rate and runway plan tied to specific milestones \u2014 how is the $4M structured relative to what it proves, and does the plan close the 'why this team can win' gap by adding commercial leadership?",
    "Founder-level discussion of the competitive landscape \u2014 specifically, what does Cadence do that ServiceTitan cannot simply add in a sprint, and why now rather than a decade ago.",
    "Full GTM breakdown: customer acquisition channel attribution for all 120 customers, CAC by channel, and explanation of how founders are spending their time on sales.",
    "Cohort-level retention data: monthly retention curves by signup cohort, going back to the earliest cohort.",
    "Churn log or summary: raw numbers on monthly and annual churn rate, and direct quotes from churned customers.",
    "Current monthly burn rate, runway, and a bottom-up headcount plan showing when they need a GTM hire.",
    "Hiring plan for commercial function: who will own sales when the founders can no longer carry it personally?",
    "Customer concentration check: top 10 customers as percentage of MRR.",
    "Competitive win/loss log: specifically, why they win against ServiceTitan and Jobber in the SMB segment, and what the churned-customer rate to those competitors looks like.",
    "Any evidence of inbound demand or pull: unsolicited referrals, waitlist, or organic channel that doesn't depend on founder relationships.",
    "Monthly cohort retention curves for all customer cohorts: GRR and NDR by cohort vintage.",
    "Fully-loaded CAC breakdown by acquisition channel \u2014 specifically, what share of the 120 customers came from founder networks versus any repeatable, scalable channel.",
